  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nVery chilly start to the weekend as high pressure dominates the \nMidwest. Clear skies and a breezy southwest winds will see wind \nchills in the twenties and teens. An abundances of sunshine combined \nwith some weak warm air advection will see these temperatures \nquickly recover back into the 50s with a few spots jumping back into \nthe 60s. Fire weather concerns continue to remain questionable, the \ngusty winds will definitely be in place across the region but how \nmuch the Northland dries out is the main dilemma. Model soundings \nhighlight a warm nose aloft with quite a bit of dry air above this \ninversion. If we manage to warm up drastically this afternoon we \ncould see some deeper mixing and drier conditions that would warrant \nsome elevate fire weather concerns.  \n\nThe pattern shifts to feature no precipitation and warming Sunday \nonward. Continued breezy southwest winds Sunday morning areawide \nwill decrease in speed from northwest to southeast during the \nafternoon. High temperatures will continue their upward climb, and \nwe will be back into the mid 60s for the next work week with \npredominantly dry weather dominating the Northland. The exception \nwill be a couple of days of lake breezes keeping shoreline areas \nright at normal for mid-October.\n\nHeading deeper into the next work week, the predominantly quiet and \nunseasonably mild weather remains locked in as an upper-level ridge \nanchors itself over the central portion of the Continent and two \ncoastal low pressure systems remain over the Mid-Atlantic and \nCalifornia/Baja region setting up a omega blocking pattern. There \nare very low- end, 10 to 20 percent, chances of light rain late next \nwork week as a ripple moving through the broad and warm upper- level \nridge may occur. However, there is very low confidence on this \nscenario at this time, and dry conditions remain heavily favored.",
